 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Everyone knows the Leaning Tower of Pisa. But a little church in Germany beats it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The tower in Suurhusen leans over five degrees. Pisa leans about four.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In 2007, Guinness even called it the most tilted tower in the world.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And the reason is hiding in the mud under it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The tower was built in the 1400s, on soggy marsh ground near the sea.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Soft ground can't hold a heavy stone tower, so the builders used tree trunks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Oak trunks, pushed down into the mud, side by side.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Under water, wood can last for centuries, because rot needs air.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And for about four hundred years, it worked.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then, in the 1800s, farmers drained the marsh to grow crops.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The water dropped, and air reached the tops of the oak trunks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The wood rotted, and one side of the tower sank into the gap.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Engineers have reinforced the base, and today it is watched closely.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Venice still stands on oak trunks too, because the lagoon keeps them wet.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Keep them wet, and they last. Dry them out, and the tower leans.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
